WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:01.480
My name's Kate Raworth.

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.040
I'm a renegade economist,

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.560
because I am passionate
about rewriting economics

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.000
so that it's fit
for the 21st Century.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:15.840
Here's the fascinating thing,

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.640
I think we're living through
a psychological drama,

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.800
in terms of what business
can be and do in the world.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.120
The 20th Century was dominated
by one narrow business mindset

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:27.960
that asked one overriding question -

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.960
how much financial value
can we extract from this?

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:36.120
But 21st Century business leaders,
designers, city designers,

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.840
they're asking a completely
different question.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.480
"How many benefits can we generate
in the way we design this?

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.040
What else can it do
for the community,

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:45.360
for culture, for the living world?

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.200
What else can it give back to
the systems of which we're are part?"

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:52.040
You see, 20th Century industry
was degenerative by design.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.680
We would take Earth's materials,
make them into things we want,

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.720
use it for a while,
and throw it away.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.920
This is a linear,
degenerative economy,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.600
it cuts against the cycles
of the living world.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:07.680
We need to bend that around so
that resources are never used up,

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.800
they're used again and again.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.480
So that we create an economy
that runs on sunlight.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.560
That waste from one process
is food for another.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.480
We work with and within the cycles
of the living world.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.360
The thing is, business alone
can't make this happen,

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:24.680
creating a circular,
regenerative economy.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.400
Imagine if every company said,

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:30.720
"Send us that shirt on your back,
that smartphone, that plastic bottle.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.640
Return it to us and we'll turn
it back into something new."

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:36.720
We'd have a loop here,
we'd have another loop over here.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:39.640
We'd have a series
of segmented loops.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:41.920
Nature would laugh at us.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.480
Nature doesn't turn a peacock
back into a peacock,

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.760
and a daffodil back into a daffodil.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:51.520
Nature creates an ecosystem
of resource use.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.880
She turns things back into building
blocks of oxygen, nitrogen, hydrogen,

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:58.240
and builds them up again.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:02.440
Regenerating life again and again,
from building blocks to an ecosystem.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.720
No one company can create
an ecosystem.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.720
We need cities and we need states.

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:08.880
We need the world to work together,

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.880
to create ecosystems of plastics
reuse, of textiles reuse,

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:14.480
of metals reuse.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.280
So that we work with and within
the cycles of the living world,

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:22.400
we mimic nature, who's been thriving
for 3.8 billion years.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.400
If we want to stick around,
we'd be wise to learn from her.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.840
Well, I never travel without
a bit of hosepipe.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.280
This came from my garden shed.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:39.220
Thanks for watching. 
Don’t forget to subscribe! :)

